 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels in the chunk, as each voxel and its 6 neighbors are checked once [S2, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(F)$ where $F$ is the number of exposed faces, which is significantly less than $6N$ [S2, unofficial].
-- **Measured Performance:** For a 226,036 voxel model, face culling reduces the triangle count to 138,260 triangles, which is 5% of the naive approach (which would render 12 triangles per voxel) [S2, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Significantly reduces draw calls and vertex count by eliminating interior geometry [S1, unofficial]. Simple to implement using neighbor checks [S3, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Does not handle transparency sorting automatically (requires two-pass methods) [S5, unofficial]. Chunk boundaries require special handling to avoid visual artifacts [S3, unofficial].
-- **When to use:** Homogeneous voxel terrain where most blocks are solid and adjacent [S1, unofficial].
-- **When not to use:** Highly sparse volumes where the overhead of neighbor checks outweighs the benefit, or when complex transparency sorting is required without additional passes [S5, unofficial].
+**Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of voxels in the chunk. Each voxel is visited once, and for each solid voxel, 6 neighbor checks are performed [S2, unofficial].
+**Memory Complexity:** $O(F)$, where $F$ is the number of exposed faces. The mesh size is proportional to the surface area of the solid voxels, not the volume [S2, unofficial].
+**Measured Performance:** For a 226,036 voxel model, face culling reduces the triangle count from 432,000+ (naive) to 138,260 triangles, which is 5% of the original count [S2, unofficial].
+**Scaling:** Performance scales with the surface area of the terrain. Highly porous or noisy terrain will have more exposed faces and thus higher mesh complexity than smooth, consolidated terrain [S2, unofficial].
+**Strengths:** Significant reduction in triangle count and draw calls compared to naive voxel rendering [S1, unofficial]. Simple to implement [S2, unofficial].
+**Limitations:** Does not reduce vertex count as much as greedy meshing [S2, unofficial]. Transparent blocks require additional passes and logic [S5, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Face Culling originated as a fundamental optimization in voxel-based game engines, particularly those inspired by Minecraft, to address the massive overdraw of rendering full cubes for every block [S3, unofficial]. The technique was formalized in early procedural terrain generation papers and implementations, which noted that naively instantiating cube models resulted in rendering 12 triangles (6 sides) when only 2 triangles (1 side) were visible [S3, unofficial]. The method involves reconstructing cubes from triangles and removing sides adjacent to solid neighbors, a process often combined with chunk-based mesh combining to manage vertex buffer limits [S3, unofficial]. It remains a core component of blocky meshing pipelines, often serving as a prerequisite for more advanced optimizations like greedy meshing [S2, unofficial].
+Face Culling, also known as Interior Polygon Removal, originated as a fundamental optimization in voxel-based game engines like Minecraft [S3, unofficial]. The technique addresses the inefficiency of rendering all 6 sides of every cube, even when 5 sides are occluded by adjacent cubes [S3, unofficial]. By reconstructing cubes from triangles and checking for solid neighbors, engines can avoid rendering 12 triangles (6 sides) when only 2 triangles (1 side) are visible [S3, unofficial]. This optimization is a core component of blocky meshing, often combined with chunk-based meshing to manage memory limits (e.g., Unity's 65,000 vertex limit per mesh) [S3, unofficial].
 
